"1. Trop Doct. 2018 Jul;48(3):192-199. doi: 10.1177/0049475517739539. Epub 2017\nNov  6.\n\nCaesarean section wound infections and antibiotic use: a retrospective \ncase-series in a tertiary referral hospital in The Gambia.\n\nAulakh A(1), Idoko P(2), Anderson ST(3), Graham W(4)(5).\n\nAuthor information:\n(1)1 Foundation Year 2 Doctor, School of Medicine and Dentistry, University of \nAberdeen, Aberdeen, UK.\n(2)2 Consultant Obstetrician and Gynaecologist, University of The Gambia Medical \nSchool, Banjul, The Gambia.\n(3)3 Head of Clinical Services, Medical Research Council Unit The Gambia, \nBanjul, The Gambia.\n(4)4 Professor of Obstetric Epidemiology, Department of Infectious Disease \nEpidemiology, 4906 London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine , London, UK.\n(5)5 Emeritus Professor of Obstetrics and Epidemiology, University of Aberdeen, \nAberdeen, UK.\n\nOurs is the first published study to examine post-Caesarean section (CS) wound \ninfections in The Gambia. We explored risk factors and clinical management \nretrospectively at a large referral hospital over a 12-month period. A total of \n777 cases were identified and records for 682 (88%) were retrieved. The CS rate \nwas 21.8% and the wound infection rate 13.2%. Risk factors included: length of \nlabour; decision-to-incision time and stillbirth. Only 7.4% of women received \npreoperative antibiotic prophylaxis, but all women received multiple-dose, \npostoperative antibiotics. The wound infection rate found is likely to be an \nunderestimate owing to loss to follow-up. The adherence to international \nguidelines regarding preoperative antibiotic prophylaxis needs to be improved.\n\nDOI: 10.1177/0049475517739539\nPMID: 29108473 [Indexed for MEDLINE]"